# dependencies
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# build output
dist/
build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# env & secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# editor / OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json

# logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# native mobile build artifacts
#
# `android/` as a whole used to be ignored, which also made it impossible to
# re-include anything inside it: git does not descend into an excluded
# DIRECTORY, so a later `!android/twa-manifest.json` is never even considered.
# Excluding the contents instead leaves that door open.
android/*
ios/

# full-length source soundtracks (~17MB) — kept local; the trimmed, compressed
# clips used by the game live in apps/client/public/music and ARE committed.
soundtracks/

# raw AI art output (large PNGs) — kept local, back these up!
# the compressed webp the game actually ships live in apps/client/public/cards
assets/raw/

# Anything dropped here to look at later and never shipped: the duplicate
# copy of the source deck, skills Or downloaded to read. Nothing in the
# game reads from it.
.scratch/

# The TWA wrapper's config IS committed — it is what makes a rebuild
# reproducible. Everything else bubblewrap generates stays out, and two of
# those files are secrets that must never reach a public repository:
# android.keystore is the upload key, and the file beside it is its password.
!android/twa-manifest.json
# And the Capacitor wrapper's, for the same reason: it is the one file the
# Android project is regenerated from, and without it a rebuild is somebody
# remembering what the version code was.
!android/capacitor.json

# And the launcher icon, for the same reason again.
#
# Or: *"the app's logo is not the Amanda logo!! Fix it so we can send it to
# the testers."* It was Capacitor's default, because nothing had ever replaced
# it. scripts/make-android-icons.mjs builds the whole set from the icon the
# website already uses — but it needs sharp, and the Android build runs with
# nothing beyond the Capacitor CLI. So the PNGs are committed and the build
# just copies them. Re-run the script when the icon changes.
!android/icons/
!android/icons/**

# Generated store assets. scripts/make-trailer.sh rebuilds the video from the
# screenshots and the score in under a minute, so a 7MB binary does not need
# to live in a public repository; store/raw/ is wherever you dropped the phone
# captures before scripts/screenshots.sh converted them.
store/trailer.mp4
store/trailer-he.mp4
store/trailer-en.mp4
store/raw/
store/raw-en/

# Single clips exported so Or can watch one shot on its own before the whole
# cut is rebuilt. Throwaway: the clip they came from lives in assets/raw.
store/preview/

# Screen recordings. Source footage is tens of megabytes and this repo is
# public; scripts/cut-gameplay.sh turns one into the seconds that ship.
#
# `/*.mp4` catches the next one dropped at the repo root, which is where a
# phone capture lands; store/recordings/ is where it gets filed, and its
# README is the only thing in there small enough to commit.
/*.mp4
/*.mov
store/gameplay.mp4
store/gameplay-en.mp4
store/recordings/*
!store/recordings/README.md

# Excel's lock file, created while a workbook is open
~$*.xlsx

# Built by scripts/make-achievements-zip.mjs — the icons it packs are already
# in the repo beside it, so the ZIP would be five megabytes of duplicate.
store/achievements/play-import/
store/achievements/play-import.zip

# Google OAuth client files land in the repo root when they are downloaded
# from the Cloud console. The Android one holds no secret — an installed
# client is identified by package name and signing fingerprint, not by a
# password — but a file called client_secret_* does not belong in a public
# repo whatever is in it, and the next one downloaded might be a web client,
# which does.
client_secret_*.json
.secrets/
